                        "entry_id": "56e7d73a-d1b5-336b-82e0-c2aaf014bbe4",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Any of several endangered medium-sized lemurs that comprise genus Propithecus."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "50258298-a0b3-49da-a442-0b6f3c27e297",
                                "sense_id": "a22378ce-835b-3beb-a1e7-3901ad3e7011",
                                "text": "Sifakas have single births, and infants ride first on their mother's front until they are about 3 months of age, then move to the mother's back, and they usually become totally independent at an age of about 6 months (Jolly 1996)."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "c903d109-82ef-47d7-b3ce-c9d0df6693af",
                                "sense_id": "a22378ce-835b-3beb-a1e7-3901ad3e7011",
                                "text": "Coquerel's sifaka (P. coquereli) is easily seen in the northwestern regions. Its chestnut-coloured arms and thighs, and piercing yellow eyes, make it particularly handsome. Like all sifakas, the young are born in June and July."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "f38899f7-7f77-4b41-a986-e01e05fad7db",
                                "sense_id": "a22378ce-835b-3beb-a1e7-3901ad3e7011",
                                "text": "Sifakas can jump about ten metres (thirty-three feet) – a little further than the human long-jump world record – which is not bad given that they are less than two foot high."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
